// SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT
pragma solidity ^0.8.25;

import {LASShake} from "../src/LASShake.sol";
import {console} from "./TwoLegSwapGas.t.sol";

/// @title LASShakeGrowth — the deterministic growth component for "does on-chain LAS
///        verification still fit one EIP-7825 transaction at Dilithium-V?"
///
/// THIS TEST STATES NO VERDICT. It emits one measurement: the marginal gas cost of a single
/// `LASShake.permute` (Keccak-f). The decision is assembled in
/// `scripts/derive_onchain_d5_bound.py`, where every subtraction is explicit and auditable.
/// The threshold depends on a client receipt and a calldata projection, neither of which is
/// knowable inside the EVM, so the two are kept apart on purpose.
///
/// WHY IT EXISTS. `LASVerifyOpt` is MEASURED to fit at Simplified Dilithium-III with 363,941
/// gas of headroom. Whether it fits at Dilithium-V was argued from arithmetic four times and
/// found unsound four times. Each defect is recorded here so the fifth attempt does not
/// reintroduce one:
///
///   1. "Execution can only grow" is not a bound. Calldata byte content is a SECOND free
///      variable; pushing it the D5-favourable way (every added byte zero) with execution
///      frozen puts D5 *under* the cap. A worst case binds only when every free variable is
///      pushed the adverse way at once.
///   2. `shake_stage_gas / block_count` does NOT lower-bound one added block: the stage total
///      also carries `init()` and the pad tail, so the quotient OVERstates per-block cost.
///   3. `SampleInBall` is not the only data-dependent stage — `_decodeZ` branches on
///      coefficient value (`if gt(f, 137935)`). Neither may be assumed to cost at least its
///      D3 value at D5, so both are subtracted whole.
///   4. `absorbPad(D5) − absorbPad(D3)` is NOT a clean lower bound either: `absorbPad`
///      allocates its 168-byte pad scratch INSIDE the frame, so memory-expansion gas lands in
///      the measurement and is larger for D5 (higher water mark), inflating the difference.
///      A loop of permutes is no better — `(g16 − g8)/8` is `permute + loop overhead`, again
///      an over-estimate. Hence the explicit unrolling below.
///
/// WHAT MAKES `permute` THE RIGHT UNIT. It is pure Keccak-f: 24 fixed rounds, fixed state,
/// only loop-counter comparisons, no allocation, no data-dependent control flow — so its cost
/// is a constant, not a distribution. And `absorbPad` calls exactly one `permute` per full
/// rate block, so 30 added full blocks cost at least 30 permutes:
///
///     preimage = pack(t) ‖ pack(w') ‖ M = 2·n·1024 + |M|,  |M| = 32 as measured on chain
///     D3 (n=6): 12,320 B → 90 full blocks + tail
///     D5 (n=8): 16,416 B → 120 full blocks + tail
///
/// The tail block exists in both and its cost differs slightly; it is simply DROPPED rather
/// than modelled, which is conservative because dropping a positive term can only weaken the
/// bound. So: SHAKE stage growth ≥ 30 × permute.
///
/// ⚠ Run WITHOUT `--gas-report`: the inspector is metered inside the measured frame and
/// inflates `gasleft()` deltas. Same rule as the cap gates in `LASGasBreakdown.t.sol`.
contract LASShakeGrowthTest {
    uint256 constant PREIMAGE_D3 = 12_320; // n = 6
    uint256 constant PREIMAGE_D5 = 16_416; // n = 8
    uint256 constant SHAKE256_RATE = 136;

    PermuteHarness h;

    function setUp() public {
        h = new PermuteHarness();
    }

    /// Differencing two EXPLICITLY UNROLLED runs. The difference is exactly eight more
    /// `permute` invocations — no loop counter, no comparison, no jump — so it is the
    /// marginal cost of the call itself, which is the form `absorbPad` pays it in.
    /// Each run is a separate external call so neither is measured against the other's
    /// memory high-water mark.
    function test_permute_unit_cost() public {
        h.permute8(); // warm-up: first permute pays any one-off scratch expansion
        h.permute16();

        uint256 g8 = h.permute8();
        uint256 g16 = h.permute16();
        require(g16 > g8, "unrolled permute did not scale");

        uint256 marginal = (g16 - g8) / 8;
        console.log("permute unrolled x8 / x16 gas", g8, g16);
        console.log("permute MARGINAL gas per call", marginal);
        console.log("30 x marginal permute (SHAKE growth lower bound)", 30 * marginal);
    }

    /// Guards the arithmetic the bound rests on: that the added FULL blocks number exactly 30,
    /// and that each length keeps a tail block (so neither needs an extra all-pad block the
    /// other does not, which would change the block accounting rather than just its cost).
    function test_block_arithmetic_holds() public pure {
        uint256 fullD3 = PREIMAGE_D3 / SHAKE256_RATE;
        uint256 fullD5 = PREIMAGE_D5 / SHAKE256_RATE;
        require(fullD5 - fullD3 == 30, "full-block delta is not 30");
        require(PREIMAGE_D3 % SHAKE256_RATE != 0, "D3 has no short tail block");
        require(PREIMAGE_D5 % SHAKE256_RATE != 0, "D5 has no short tail block");
        console.log("full rate blocks D3 / D5", fullD3, fullD5);
        console.log("tail bytes D3 / D5", PREIMAGE_D3 % SHAKE256_RATE, PREIMAGE_D5 % SHAKE256_RATE);
    }
}

/// Separate contract so each measurement is an external call with its own memory space.
